import * from net;

mod export sql;

// DOCS -- public `sql` module (UNIFIED_CORE_LIBRARY.md §4). This is the
// portable front of the generic SQL client
// library. It is a PROTOCOL module (§4 rule 7): it contains ZERO OS code --
// no win32 imports, no `#on` regions, no os module gates -- and every byte
// on the wire is sent through the universal socket
// core std/net.sr (connectRaw/sendRawLen/recvRaw/closeRaw/
// setRecvTimeoutRaw), whose Windows regions hold the ws2_32 calls. This
// module is pure SQL-transport logic and would compile unchanged on any
// backend that provides net.
//
// TRANSPORT: connect(host, port) is net.connectRaw(host, port) followed by
// net.setRecvTimeoutRaw(sock, 30000); the int[] packing send is
// net.sendRawLen(sock, line, line.length + 2); the receive is
// net.recvRaw(sock, 4096); the close is net.closeRaw(sock).
// net.recvRaw normalizes the DLL return
// first and then reports "" for `n <= 0` -- the same observable graceful
// shape a raw `n <= 0 || n > 2147483647` check produces (both compilers).
// No SQL wire protocol of any kind exists here or anywhere
// (see the honesty note below) -- the module sends the SQL text as one
// CRLF-terminated line per statement and surfaces the raw reply through
// getLastReply().
//
// FAILURE STYLE: execute/query are silent no-ops that leave
// getLastReply() "" and return an empty result whenever the connStr names no
// reachable Server/Port, close() stays a no-op.
//
// DISPATCH (per compiler, probed): `sql` is builtin-reserved on NEITHER
// compiler -- silverc-rs's builtin_modules_set (src/main.rs:81 lists
// fs/json/yaml/env/url/hash/path/bom/console/math/http only) and the
// self-hosted compiler's visBuiltinModule (compiler/visibility.sr:605 lists
// fs/json/yaml/llmai/LLMAI/os/project/exception/bom/process/notifications/
// env/url/hash/path/console/math/http) plus its driver prepend list all
// exclude it. Both compilers resolve `import * from sql;` as an ORDINARY
// SOURCE module (std/sql.sr); no native dispatch exists.
// (sql.sr's entry in silverc.srp's exclude list only keeps it out of the
// compiler's own aggregate build -- the same posture as smtp.sr/pop.sr/
// imap.sr/ssh.sr; it does not make `sql` a builtin.)
//
// HONESTY NOTE -- this is NOT a SQL client for any real server: there is NO
// SQL wire protocol here and none is invented. Real SQL servers each speak
// their own protocol (MySQL handshake, Postgres startup message, TDS, the
// SQLite file format -- the last not even a socket protocol), and no
// framing for any of them exists in this file. A real implementation for
// one dialect is a separate feature ticket. What this module implements is
// exactly the transport: the
// connStr's `Server=`/`Host=` and `Port=` keys are parsed with the pure
// connValue/connHost/connPort helpers (ODBC-standard connection-string keys
// -- string parsing, NOT a wire protocol), each execute()/query() opens one
// real TCP connection (ssh exec()'s per-call shape), sends the SQL text as a
// single CRLF-terminated line, reads the reply bounded (16 x 4096 bytes with
// a 30 s SO_RCVTIMEO -- no path can hang), closes the socket and surfaces
// the raw reply bytes through getLastReply(). result.rows stay
// EMPTY on this build -- there is no row framing to interpret the reply
// with, so hasNext() is false and next() null; parsing peer bytes into rows
// would be inventing a protocol. Against a fake listener (or a real server
// that answers with junk) the junk (or "" after a silent disconnect) lands
// in getLastReply() and the process exits cleanly. No exceptions anywhere.
// The host in the connStr must be a dotted IP (inet_addr posture -- no name
// resolution, same as std/ssh.sr's client); a missing/empty Server or Port
// fails the connect gracefully (execute/query become silent no-ops).
//
// OVERLOAD DISPATCH BUG (compiler-level, probed on BOTH compilers
// with identical results -- reported here so the compiler team can pick it
// up): same-name METHOD
// overloads with DIFFERENT ARITIES dispatch by NAME to the LAST-DECLARED
// overload, ignoring arity. Evidence (standalone probes, both compilers):
// with `exec(string)` declared before `exec(string, object[])`, the ONE-arg
// call `c.exec("A")` printed "exec2:A" (the two-arg body ran); with the
// declaration order reversed, the TWO-arg call `c.exec("B", [])` printed
// "first1:B" (the one-arg body ran). A same-name delegation inside an
// overload body (`this.query(sql)` inside the two-arg query) therefore
// re-dispatches to ITSELF and recurses forever. (This also explains why
// std/ssh.sr's authWithKey(string,string)/authWithKey(string,string,string)
// pair "probes working": both bodies are empty no-ops, so the mis-dispatch
// stays invisible.) HOW THIS FILE HANDLES IT (no compiler change): the two-arg
// execute/query overloads do NOT delegate to this.execute/this.query -- each
// calls a differently-named private implementation (runStatement/runQuery)
// and carries its params unused, so both overloads of a name are
// behaviorally identical and the name-only dispatch lands safely whichever
// overload it picks.
//
// ARRAY-LENGTH LANDMINE (self-host): the `.length()` CALL
// form on CLASS-element arrays (row[] and any user-class element type) reads
// garbage on the self-hosted compiler (probed: an empty `= []`-assigned
// elemD[] field reported 9442192; a 1-element local reported 18 then 19;
// silverc-rs reported the correct 0/1 on the same sources), while the
// `.length` PROPERTY form is correct on BOTH compilers (the std/guard.sr
// posture -- this.vars.length everywhere). This file uses the property form
// for row[] exclusively (string[] keeps the
// call form where csv.sr precedent uses it -- the breakage is specific to
// class-element arrays).
//
// KVP LANDMINE (probed): an UNINITIALIZED `kvp<K,V>` field is not
// usable -- rs silently swallows set() and get() returns null; the self-host
// access-violates on the first set() (probed via std/headers.sr's multimap,
// which carries the same uninitialized-kvp latent bug -- NOT fixed here,
// that file is out of this task's scope, as is std/sqlite.sr's copy of the
// same pattern). This file's row uses two parallel
// `= []`-initialized string[] arrays (the guard.sr/csv-writer posture) and
// keeps get()'s contract: the matching value, or null when the name is
// absent (the kvp.get contract).
//
// PUBLIC API: class row -- construct(), get(string) -> string; class result
// -- construct(), hasNext() -> bool, next() -> row; class connection --
// construct(string), execute(string), execute(string, object[]), query(string)
// -> result, query(string, object[]) -> result, close(); free function
// connect(string) -> connection. Every name (row, result, connection, get,
// hasNext, next, execute, query, close, connect) is keyword-free on both
// compilers -- checked against silverc-rs
// src/token.rs (read_identifier_or_keyword, lines 427-495) and the self-host
// compiler/lexer.sr (keywordKind, lines 656-718). The dangerous SQL-adjacent
// identifiers are clear: `from`/`to`/`match` are keywords on both and appear
// nowhere in this API; `select`/`insert` are keywords on NEITHER and are not
// member names here anyway (SQL text is caller-supplied data, not API).
// `object[]` params survive as-is (the type parses on both compilers --
// probed). The declaration ORDER of the overload pairs is kept
// (one-arg first, two-arg second), which under the dispatch bug means the
// two-arg bodies serve every call shape -- they are behaviorally identical
// to the one-arg bodies by construction (see the dispatch bug note).
//
// ADDITIONAL SURFACE (the std/ssh.sr precedent): connection.getLastReply()
// returns the raw text of the last reply bytes read from the peer (""
// before the first statement, after a failed connect, or when the peer said
// nothing); row.has(name) is the containment test (get returns null for
// absent names, so has() is the non-null check callers want); and four free
// pure helpers -- escapeLiteral(v) (SQL string literal: single-quoted with
// internal single quotes doubled, the SQL-standard doubling only -- no
// dialect-specific backslash escapes invented), escapeIdentifier(name)
// (double-quoted, internal double quotes doubled), connValue(connStr, key)
// (the value of the first `key=value;` pair, keys compared
// case-insensitively, "" when absent) and its wrappers connHost(connStr)
// (Server, falling back to Host) and connPort(connStr) (Port as a decimal,
// 0 when absent/non-numeric/> 65535) -- are pure and probeable
// byte-identically on both compilers. Also invisible internals: the
// private lastReply field (initialized in construct), the private
// runStatement/runQuery/readBounded
// plumbing, and construct() also initializes row's arrays.
// Method calls inside this class ALWAYS carry an explicit `this.`: probed on
// silverc-rs, an unqualified call to a private METHOD (readBounded inside
// runStatement) is resolved as a FREE function and emitted UNRESOLVED
// ("unresolved call(s) trap at runtime: readBounded [in class connection]
// [in fn runStatement]").
//
// CALLER NOTES: instantiate with `var c = connect(...);` -- a lowercase class
// arriving from a star-imported module cannot be spelled as a local's type on
// silverc-rs (per-file parsing; `var` works on both, probed via std/ftps.sr)
// and `new mod.connection()` dies on BOTH compilers. Strings returned across
// this module's boundary (row.get, row.has is bool, getLastReply and the
// free helpers) should be assigned to a local before being concatenated into
// larger expressions (silverc-rs stringifies the return pointer in
// expression position; see std/dns.sr's RS-ONLY CALLER LANDMINE). next()
// returns null once exhausted (and always on this build -- rows stay empty
// until a real protocol lands); row.get returns null for absent names --
// check row.has() first. There is NO session: every statement is one
// connect/send/read/close cycle (the ssh exec() shape) and close() is a
// no-op (nothing to close between statements).

// Escapes `v` as an SQL string literal: wrapped in single quotes with every
// internal single quote doubled (the SQL-standard doubling; dialect-specific
// backslash escapes are NOT invented). Pure; probeable
// byte-identically on both compilers.
public string escapeLiteral(string v) {
    string outS = "'";
    for (int i = 0; i < v.length; i++) {
        if (v[i] == 39) {
            outS += "''";
        } else {
            outS += v[i];
        }
    }
    outS += "'";
    return outS;
}

// Escapes `name` as an SQL identifier: wrapped in double quotes with every
// internal double quote doubled. Pure; probeable.
public string escapeIdentifier(string name) {
    string outS = "\"";
    for (int i = 0; i < name.length; i++) {
        if (name[i] == 34) {
            outS += "\"\"";
        } else {
            outS += name[i];
        }
    }
    outS += "\"";
    return outS;
}

// The value of the first `key=value;` pair in the ODBC-style connection
// string (keys compared case-insensitively), "" when the key is absent.
// Pure; probeable.
public string connValue(string connStr, string key) {
    int n = connStr.length;
    string keyL = key.toLower();
    int i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        int j = i;
        while (j < n && connStr[j] != 61 && connStr[j] != 59) { j++; }
        if (j >= n) { break; }
        if (connStr[j] == 59) { i = j + 1; continue; }
        string tok = sliceRange(connStr, i, j).toLower();
        if (tok == keyL) {
            int m = j + 1;
            while (m < n && connStr[m] != 59) { m++; }
            return sliceRange(connStr, j + 1, m);
        }
        i = j + 1;
    }
    return "";
}

// The connection string's host: its `Server=` value, falling back to
// `Host=`; "" when neither is present (the caller's connect then fails
// gracefully). Pure; probeable.
public string connHost(string connStr) {
    string v = connValue(connStr, "server");
    if (v.length > 0) { return v; }
    return connValue(connStr, "host");
}

// The connection string's `Port=` value as a decimal; 0 when absent,
// non-numeric, or above 65535 (an invalid port fails the connect
// gracefully). Pure; probeable.
public int connPort(string connStr) {
    string v = connValue(connStr, "port");
    int n = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < v.length; i++) {
        int c = int(v[i]);
        if (c < 48 || c > 57) { return 0; }
        n = n * 10 + (c - 48);
        if (n > 65535) { return 0; }
    }
    return n;
}

class row {
    private string[] names;
    private string[] values;

    construct() {
        this.names = [];
        this.values = [];
    }

    // The value stored under `name`, or null when absent
    // (the kvp.get contract). On this
    // build rows are never populated (no row framing exists -- see the
    // honesty note), so every get() returns null; the parallel-array
    // implementation exists so a future protocol can fill rows without an
    // API change.
    public string get(string name) {
        for (int i = 0; i < this.names.length; i++) {
            if (this.names[i] == name) {
                return this.values[i];
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    // True when `name` is present (get returns null for absent
    // names, so has() is the non-null check callers want).
    public bool has(string name) {
        for (int i = 0; i < this.names.length; i++) {
            if (this.names[i] == name) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}

class result {
    private row[] rows;
    private int idx;

    construct() {
        this.idx = 0;
        this.rows = [];
    }

    // Row iteration: the `.length` PROPERTY form (the
    // .length() CALL form reads garbage on the self-host for class-element
    // arrays -- see the ARRAY-LENGTH landmine; the call form also breaks on
    // a never-initialized array -- a null-deref besides). False on
    // this build (rows stay empty until a real protocol lands).
    public bool hasNext() {
        return this.idx < this.rows.length;
    }

    // The row at the cursor, advancing it; the guard returns null once
    // exhausted (and always on this build).
    public row next() {
        if (this.idx >= this.rows.length) { return null; }
        row r = this.rows[this.idx];
        this.idx++;
        return r;
    }
}

class connection {
    private string connStr;
    private string lastReply;

    construct(string connStr) {
        this.connStr = connStr;
        this.lastReply = "";
    }

    // The raw text of the
    // last reply bytes read from the peer -- "" before the first statement,
    // after a failed connect, or when the peer said nothing and closed.
    // This is the graceful-failure surface: against a non-SQL listener it
    // holds the junk the peer sent.
    public string getLastReply() {
        return this.lastReply;
    }

    // One per-statement connect /
    // send / bounded read / close cycle (see the honesty note -- the SQL
    // text goes out as one CRLF-terminated line and the raw reply, if any,
    // lands in getLastReply()). Silent no-op when the connStr names no
    // reachable Server/Port. NOTE (overload dispatch bug): call sites
    // dispatch by NAME to the last-declared overload of a name regardless of
    // arity; both overloads call the same private runStatement, so either
    // landing is behaviorally identical.
    public void execute(string sql) {
        this.runStatement(sql);
    }

    // Two-arg execute; params are carried unused (no client-side
    // substitution). Deliberately does NOT call this.execute(sql) -- that
    // delegation re-dispatches to the last-declared same-name overload
    // (itself) and recurses forever on both compilers (see the dispatch bug
    // note).
    public void execute(string sql, object[] params) {
        this.runStatement(sql);
    }

    // Runs the statement like execute() and
    // returns a result whose rows stay EMPTY on this build (no row framing
    // exists to interpret the reply -- see the honesty note); the raw reply
    // is in getLastReply().
    public result query(string sql) {
        return this.runQuery(sql);
    }

    // Two-arg query; params carried unused, no same-name
    // delegation (see the dispatch bug note).
    public result query(string sql, object[] params) {
        return this.runQuery(sql);
    }

    // No-op: there is no session socket
    // to close -- every statement owns one connect/send/read/close cycle and
    // closes it before returning.
    public void close() { }

    // Shared implementation of both execute overloads (differently-named on
    // purpose -- the dispatch bug note above). One per-statement connect /
    // send / bounded read / close cycle through the net primitives (see the
    // TRANSPORT note): connectRaw to the connStr's host:port, the 30 s
    // receive timeout armed right after, the SQL line sent CRLF-terminated
    // with sendRawLen's explicit length, the
    // bounded reply read, closeRaw before returning. Silent no-op when the
    // connStr names no reachable Server/Port.
    private void runStatement(string sql) {
        this.lastReply = "";
        string host = connHost(this.connStr);
        int port = connPort(this.connStr);
        int sock = net.connectRaw(host, port);
        if (sock < 0) { return; }
        net.setRecvTimeoutRaw(sock, 30000);
        string line = sql + "\r\n";
        net.sendRawLen(sock, line, line.length + 2);
        this.lastReply = this.readBounded(sock);
        net.closeRaw(sock);
    }

    // Shared implementation of both query overloads.
    private result runQuery(string sql) {
        this.runStatement(sql);
        result r = new result();
        return r;
    }

    // Bounded reply read: accumulates reads until the peer goes quiet or
    // 16 x 4096 bytes (~64 KB) pass -- whichever comes first. The 30 s
    // SO_RCVTIMEO armed after net.connectRaw bounds each individual read,
    // so no path can spin forever.
    private string readBounded(int sock) {
        string acc = "";
        for (int guard = 0; guard < 16; guard++) {
            string chunk = net.recvRaw(sock, 4096);
            if (chunk.length <= 0) { break; }
            acc += chunk;
        }
        return acc;
    }
}

// Wraps the connStr in a connection.
// No network happens here -- connections happen
// per-statement (see the honesty note).
public connection connect(string connStr) {
    return new connection(connStr);
}

// Copies s[start .. end) character by character. Deliberately NOT substr:
// rs's two-arg substr treats X as an END offset while the self-host treats
// it as a LENGTH -- identical only when start is 0 (probed; see
// silverc-rs/src/string_ops.rs emit_string_substring) -- and every caller
// here slices from a nonzero offset.
private string sliceRange(string s, int start, int end) {
    string result = "";
    for (int i = start; i < end; i++) {
        result += s[i];
    }
    return result;
}
